        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The primary goals of this research have been to determine the signaling components present in plant roots that allow for both gravity and touch sensitivity. In addition, we have sought to define how these elements may interact to allow the plant to integrate multiple stimuli to generate the most appropriate growth response to an ever-changing environment rich in multiple, often conflicting stimuli.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>

Touch and gravity are two of the many stimuli that plants must integrate to generate an appropriate growth response. Due to the mechanical nature of both of these signals, shared signal transduction elements could well form the basis of the cross-talk between these two sensory systems. Two of the most widely proposed candidate signal transduction elements in both plant touch and gravity sensing are changes in Ca2+ and pH in the cytoplasm of the sensory cells. However, the precise spatial and temporal characteristics of changes in these signaling elements in response to mechanical and/or gravity stimulation, and especially how they might interact in a signaling cross-talk system, are poorly defined. The overall goal of this experimental program has therefore been to seek to define the spatial and temporal character of the ionic signaling associated with mechano-perception. Using this characterization we then mimicked or blocked the appropriate dynamics of these ionic changes and so helped define how they regulate the signaling systems responsible for the gravitropic response and generation of plant form.
Touch stimulation has dramatic effects on plant morphogenesis and its interaction with gravimorphogenesis has a critical role in determining overall plant development and productivity. Thus, understanding the molecular mechanisms behind touch and gravity sensing systems and how these interact to generate plant form will not only contribute to our fundamental understanding of how plants grow, but may also suggest avenues for novel approaches to engineering crop stature or growth habit. In addition, it may be possible to manipulate the gravity response system of the plant with appropriate mechanical stimulation to circumvent problems of plant growth in the weightless environment of space flight.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
The growth responses elicited by both these touch- and gravity- responsive systems appear intimately connected to the plant hormone auxin. Thus, we have found that prolonging the gravity signal generated in the sensory cells of the root cap by disrupting actin, as described above, leads to a significant increase in auxin export from these sensory cells to those in the region of the root undergoing growth regulation. Therefore, in collaboration with Roberto Gaxiola (University of Connecticut), we have explored some of the driving forces for such auxin transport that operate in addition to the well-recognized roles of the PIN, PGP and AUX/LAX families of auxin transporters. We have determined that the vacuolar proton pumping pyrophosphatase (AVP1) also plays a role in root growth responses. This protein appears to sustain the hormonal fluxes required for integration of growth, likely through regulation of proton fluxes in the vesicles that correctly target the auxin transport systems and primary proton pumps of the plant to the plasma membrane. The novel role for this protein was revealed from analysis of growth and pH dynamics in the vacuole, cytoplasm and wall of plants disrupted in AVP1 production or over-expressing avp1. AVP1 therefore provides another molecular target for manipulation of plant growth responses to both the touch and gravity signal.
        </div>

